{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791434104,"nonce":"FglGT64bbY9XuJP5nBEmtXzM18g=","title":"Unsafe casts from boxed array elements lose scalar context","message":"On 04ab5b71329d6adf05a0c4bec27ecb460bd35238, an Any array slot cast with var result:Float = cast values[1] is typed as Dynamic instead of Float. The Any slot carries a boxed pointer in an i64 register. The outer variable conversion then converts that address numerically instead of extracting the Float payload. The empty-array-element fix makes the independent cast failure visible after the array length is repaired.\n\nAn untyped cast should take the scalar type of its initializer, argument, or return context. Current AST lowering adopts class, interface, type-parameter and abstract contexts, but omits Int, Float, Bool and String. Native Haxe accepts these scalar contexts.\n\nContextualArrayCasts.hx covers an Any array's Int, Float, Bool and String slots, plus Float casts in function return and argument positions. Native Haxe passes. The v3 prototype adds those scalar contextual targets; pinned and corpus verification are pending.\n\nThe existing AnyValues.hx regression passes with implicit Any extraction, and ContextualUnsafeCasts.hx with direct scalar Dynamic/Any variables also passes: the failure specifically exposes the loss of representation evidence after an array load.\n\nArtifacts: /tmp/rayzor-empty-array-mir-v1.log (i64 array slot converted directly to f64), /tmp/rayzor-array-elements-pinned-v1.log, /tmp/rayzor-contextual-array-casts-native-v3.log.","files":null}]}